      <encodingDesc>
         <appInfo>
            <application xml:id="a2v331c" isodate="2025-08-29T17:43:47" version="5.5.0-e003172">
               <name xml:id="n1d8hhm9">Verovio</name>
               <p xml:id="pzcxrin">Transcoded from MusicXML</p>
            </application>
            <application startdate="2026-06-29T16:14:32" enddate="2026-10-24T23:25:37" version="1.4.2">
               <name>mei-friend</name>
               <p>Manual correction of the source pages. Dates are the earliest start and latest end recorded in the page files for BSB images 00089-00099; the version is the latest recorded (1.3.0 in 00090, 1.4.2 in the others).</p>
            </application>
         </appInfo>
         <editorialDecl>
            <p>This is a working corrected digital edition based on DdT 1903, assembled on 2026-10-25 from the proofread page-level encodings of BSB images 00089-00099 (printed pp. 79-89) at repository revision 176a2557. OMR and conversion errors are corrected against the DdT facsimile; correction credits follow the page-file history for Francy Häring, Jan-Raphael Kemnitzer and Egor Polyakov. The Op. II historical print was consulted selectively through the STIMU 1991 facsimile of the Uppsala partbooks for the gamba E4 at measure 114 (printed p. 4, fifth staff; scan-0027, left-hand page) on 2026-10-25. This selected comparison does not constitute complete collation with the historical print. Substantive departures from DdT are documented in editorial annotations recording the source reading, encoded reading, evidence or conjectural reasoning, and responsible editor. Source-supported and conjectural emendations are distinguished.</p>
            <p>The staff arrangement and keyboard realization printed in DdT are retained. Staff 1 is violin; staff 2 is viola da gamba; staff 3 is the figured continuo bass; staves 4 and 5 are the upper and lower staves of the printed keyboard realization. The realization is distinguished from the figured continuo bass when defining an analytical view. Changes of clef, key and meter are preserved; redundant page-opening signatures are omitted to avoid added courtesy signs.</p>
            <p>Original page and system boundaries are represented by pb and sb elements. Surface and page-break numbers form a work-local sequence beginning with 1; the printed page range is recorded in sourceDesc. Each measure points to a measure zone on the corresponding facsimile surface using @facs. In assembly, eight within-page pb markers of the page files became system breaks, consistent with the facsimile zones, so that all 43 systems are retained; measures and zones are numbered continuously. The final measure 203 is page-local measure 18 of image 00099, separated on 2026-10-22 from the preceding bar, with which the import had fused it. Existing event IDs are retained except for cross-page collisions, which receive page-qualified IDs with their local references updated. Inherited MusicXML ppq, dur.ppq and accid.ges attributes are removed following the CAMAT page-combination workflow; written durations, written accidentals and key signatures are retained.</p>
            <p>Three departures from DdT are documented in editorial annotations. At measure 114 the gamba E4 is encoded as a dotted whole, a source-supported emendation of the undotted whole in DdT: editorial.op2.01.m114.e4-dot cites the Uppsala Op. II print through STIMU 1991 and links a supplemental facsimile excerpt. Two conjectural emendations of the keyboard realization rest on DdT alone. At measure 90 the late F4 in the lower voice of the upper keyboard staff is an eighth on the final eighth-note subdivision, not the quarter it appears to be in DdT (editorial.op2.01.m090.f4). At measure 163 the opening A-flat4 in the upper voice of the upper keyboard staff is a dotted quarter, where DdT appears undotted, restoring 6/4 and the alignment of the voices (editorial.op2.01.m163.aflat4-dot). No historical-witness support is claimed for these two readings.</p>
         </editorialDecl>
         <projectDesc>
            <p>This edition was prepared by CAMAT Corpus Editions as part of a DFG-funded corpus for music-analysis technologies (grant PF 669/18-1).</p>
         </projectDesc>
      </encodingDesc>
      <workList>
