                     <tie xml:id="t1ugd38w" startid="#s4edr35" endid="#h41vz2t"/>
                     <tie xml:id="f13gu8wr" startid="#e1b5u41r" endid="#b3xso5p"/>
                     <annot xml:id="editorial.op2.01.m114.e4-dot" type="editorial" label="source-supported-emendation" plist="#wmjri1r" resp="#resp.polyakov" source="#src.uppsala.op2">
                        <p><ref target="#src.ddt1903">DdT 1903</ref>, printed p. 85 (BSB image 95), measure 114, viola da gamba, gives E4 as an undotted whole note. A dot is supplied here, encoding a dotted whole, as proposed by Egor Polyakov on 2026-10-25 and attested by the Op. II print of 1696 consulted through the <ref target="#src.stimu1991.op2">STIMU 1991 facsimile of the Uppsala partbooks</ref>: Viola da Gamba part, Sonata I, Grave, printed p. 4, fifth staff, supplied scan-0027.jpg, left-hand page. The corresponding E4 has a visible augmentation dot, after the preceding C4 dotted half and D4 quarter and before the following D4 whole. The historical print groups the passages numbered 113 and 114 in DdT into one printed bar; the supplemental excerpt shows that complete bar between its two barlines. This passage was compared directly on 2026-10-25; the source-supported dotted whole fills all six quarter-note beats of 3/2, whereas DdT's undotted whole fills only four.</p>
                        <p><ref type="supplemental-material" target="../supplemental_material/supplemental_material_op2_01_m114_stimu1991.png">Supplemental facsimile excerpt: Op. II/1, complete historical-print bar corresponding to DdT measures 113–114, viola da gamba, ending with E4 dotted whole (1696 print through STIMU 1991).</ref></p>
                     </annot>
                  </measure>
